---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import JsonMinifier from '../../components/JsonMinifier.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="JSON Minifier" description="Strip whitespace from JSON to make it as compact as possible for transfer or storage.">
  <JsonMinifier client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>Why minify JSON</h2>
  <p>Removing whitespace reduces payload size for network transfer or storage — useful for config files, API responses, or embedding JSON in a URL parameter.</p>

  <h2>Common JSON Minification Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Minifying JSON meant for human editing</strong>, like a source config file that developers regularly need to read and modify.</li>
    <li><strong>Not realizing gzip compression often matters more</strong> than minification for real transfer size — most production APIs benefit more from compression than from removing whitespace alone.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This JSON Minifier</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Reducing payload size for a JSON string embedded in a URL</li>
    <li>Compacting a config file for production where readability doesn't matter</li>
    <li>Quickly minifying JSON for a size comparison</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>JSON Minification FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does minifying JSON change its meaning?</strong> No — minification only removes whitespace and formatting; the actual data structure and values are completely unchanged.</p>
  <p><strong>Should I minify JSON before gzip compression?</strong> It rarely helps much — gzip already compresses repetitive whitespace very effectively, so the combined size of minified+gzipped and formatted+gzipped JSON is often nearly identical.</p>
</ToolLayout>
